course: world
lesson: 77
title: Fungi, bacteria and tiny living things
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Fungi are not plants: they have no green chlorophyll and cannot make their own food, so they feed on dead or living things around them; mushrooms, moulds and yeast are all fungi.
explain: The mushroom we see is only the fruit of a fungus; most of it is a web of fine threads hidden in the soil or wood, and the mushroom spreads tiny spores instead of seeds.
explain: Bacteria are living things made of just one tiny cell, far too small to see without a microscope; they live everywhere — in soil, water, air and inside our bodies.
explain: Most bacteria are harmless or helpful: they rot dead things, help us digest food and turn milk into yogurt; only a few cause illness, and yeast makes bread rise by giving off bubbles of gas.
explain: Leave a slice of bread in a warm, damp place and in a few days fuzzy spots of mould appear — spores from the air landed on it and the fungus grew by feeding on the bread.
fact: Fungi cannot make their own food because they have no chlorophyll.
fact: Mushrooms, moulds and yeast are all fungi.
fact: A mushroom is the part of a fungus that makes spores.
fact: Most of a fungus is a web of thin threads hidden in soil or wood.
fact: Fungi spread by spores, not by seeds.
fact: Bacteria are living things made of only one cell.
fact: Bacteria are too small to see without a microscope.
fact: Bacteria in our gut help us to digest food.
fact: Bacteria turn milk into yogurt and cheese.
fact: Milk left out for days goes sour because bacteria grow in it.
fact: Fungi and bacteria grow best where it is warm and damp; the cold of a fridge or freezer slows them almost to a stop.
fact: Yeast makes bread rise by giving off bubbles of carbon dioxide.
fact: Fungi and bacteria break down dead plants and animals and return their goodness to the soil.
fact: Some wild mushrooms are deadly poisonous, so we must never eat ones we pick ourselves.
exercise: Why are fungi not plants? | They have no chlorophyll and cannot make food | They are too small to be plants | They only grow in the dark | They have no roots or leaves at all, only a stalk and a cap
exercise: Which of these is a fungus? | Mould on old bread | Moss on a rock | Grass in a field | Seaweed in the sea
exercise: How do fungi spread? | By tiny spores | By seeds in fruit | By flowers and bees | By runners on the ground
exercise: Where is most of a fungus? | In thin threads under the soil or in wood | In the mushroom cap that we can see above the ground | In the air around them as a thin cloud of dust | In the roots of nearby trees and nowhere else
exercise: How many cells does a bacterium have? | One | Ten | A thousand | Millions
exercise: How can we see bacteria? | With a microscope | With a magnifying glass only | With our eyes in bright light | We can never see them at all
exercise: Which food is made with the help of bacteria? | Yogurt | Apple juice | Boiled rice | Salt
exercise: What makes bread dough rise? | Gas bubbles from yeast | Steam from cold water | Air from the flour bag | Heat from the salt
exercise: Where do bacteria live? | Almost everywhere, even inside us | Only in dirty places like bins and drains | Only in hot countries | Only in sick people
exercise: Which is true about most bacteria? | Most are harmless or helpful | All of them cause illness | All of them live in rubbish | Most of them are as big as ants
exercise: How do fungi help a forest? | They break down dead wood and leaves | They make their own food from sunlight | They catch insects to feed the forest birds | They pump water up from the soil into trees
exercise: Why should you never eat a wild mushroom you pick? | Some mushrooms are deadly poisonous | Wild mushrooms have no taste | Mushrooms are not real food | Wild mushrooms are too hard
exercise: Why does mould grow fast on bread in a warm, damp place? | Fungi grow best with warmth and moisture | The bread turns into mould by itself | Cold air brings the mould spores | Bread is made of mould already
exercise: What do the bacteria in our gut do? | Help us digest food | Make our bones grow | Pump our blood | Help us see in the dark
test: Why does keeping bread in the freezer stop mould? | The cold slows the fungus almost to a stop | The cold kills all the flour so there is nothing left to eat | Mould only grows in sunlight | Freezers make bread poisonous
test: Which is true about a mushroom you see in a wood? | It is only a small part of a much bigger fungus | It is the whole living fungus | It is a kind of green plant | It grew from a seed dropped by a bird
test: A cook forgets to add yeast to bread dough. What will happen? | The bread will stay flat | The bread will rise even more | The bread will turn green | The bread will taste sweeter
test: What would happen in a forest if there were no fungi or bacteria? | Dead leaves and logs would pile up and not rot | Trees would grow twice as fast with nothing to eat their leaves | The soil would become richer | Nothing at all would change
test: Why do we wash our hands even though most bacteria are harmless? | A few bacteria can make us ill | All bacteria are dangerous | Bacteria make our hands heavy | Washing feeds the good bacteria
test: Which living thing is the smallest? | A bacterium | A mushroom | An ant | A grain of mould you can see
test: A pupil says mushrooms are plants because they grow from the ground. What is the mistake? | Fungi cannot make their own food like plants | Mushrooms do not grow from the ground | Plants never grow from the ground | Mushrooms are really animals
test: How is a spore like a seed? | Both can grow into a new living thing | Both are made inside a flower and need bees | Both are big enough to hold in your hand | Both are made by bacteria living in the soil
test: Milk left out for days turns sour. What caused it? | Bacteria growing in the milk | The milk turning into water | Light changing its colour | The cup making it sour
test: why'd the bread go all fuzzy and green | Mould, a fungus, grew on it from tiny spores | The bread turned green as it dried out on the shelf | Germs in the air painted it green | The flour went bad and grew grass
test: how does yeast make bread puff up | Yeast gives off bubbles of carbon dioxide | Yeast soaks up water and swells | Yeast melts in the oven and fills with air | Yeast makes the flour grow bigger in the heat
test: Leo says all bacteria are bad germs. true? | No, gut bacteria help us digest and bacteria make yogurt and cheese | Yes, every kind of bacteria makes people ill | Yes, but only the bacteria you can see | No, bacteria are only found in dirty water
test: my cousin reckons mushrooms are plants cos they grow in the ground. right? | No, mushrooms are fungi and cannot make their own food | Yes, mushrooms are plants without leaves | Yes, they make food from sunlight like plants | No, mushrooms are tiny animals living in soil
exam: Identify the principal reason for which fungi are incapable of manufacturing their own food. | They possess no chlorophyll | They possess no roots | They grow only in darkness | They are composed of a single cell
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the means by which fungi spread? | By spores | By seeds | By bulbs | By runners
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning bacteria. | They are living things composed of one cell | They are visible to the unaided eye | They are all invariably harmful | They are small fungi bearing spores
exam: Ascertain the cause of the rising of bread dough. | Yeast gives off bubbles of carbon dioxide | Bacteria give off bubbles of oxygen | The flour expands upon contact with water | The salt draws air into the dough
